# Copy this to `.env` before running `docker compose up`.
# Defaults are reasonable for a single-host install.
#
# Running a SECOND Sill on this host: every commented-out name below has to
# change, not just SILL_DB_CONTAINER. docker-compose.yml names four
# containers and binds three host ports, and the Compose project name (which
# prefixes the volume names) defaults to the directory it runs from —
# `backend`, identically for every clone. See the README's "Run a second Sill
# side by side". install.sh, verify.sh, and upgrade.sh all read this file.

# --- Compose project (volume-name prefix) ---
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=sill

# --- Restart policy ---
# All four services default to `unless-stopped`: they come back after a
# reboot, a Docker/OrbStack engine restart, or a crash, but stay down if you
# deliberately ran `docker compose stop`.
#
# This defaults ON because the failure it prevents is invisible. With no
# policy, one engine restart leaves the store absent, and the
# spontaneous-recall hook degrades exactly as designed — a bare [TIME] header
# and no memories — which looks identical to "nothing relevant was found".
# Observed on a real install: four containers exited during an OrbStack
# restart and nothing surfaced it for over an hour.
#
# Set to `no` if you would rather start the stack by hand.
# SILL_RESTART_POLICY=unless-stopped

# --- Network exposure ---
# Which host interface the published ports bind to. Default 0.0.0.0 is every
# interface, which is what this file has always produced.
#
# Worth a deliberate decision rather than a default, because of what is behind
# those ports: the database runs with POSTGRES_HOST_AUTH_METHOD=trust, so it
# asks for no password at all — anything that can reach 5432 is a superuser on
# the entire memory store, and the db image carries pgsql-http, so that also
# means outbound HTTP from inside the container network. RabbitMQ's management
# UI on 15672 has known credentials sitting a few lines below this comment.
#
# Nothing in a single-host install needs those ports off-box: the hooks reach
# Postgres by `docker exec`, and the MCP server connects to localhost. So
# unless another machine genuinely has to connect, bind to loopback:
#
#   SILL_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
#
# Left at the default, an untrusted network (a cafe, a conference, a shared
# office) is a network on which your memory store is readable and writable by
# anyone on it.
# SILL_BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0

# --- Postgres ---
# Container name lets multiple Sill instances coexist (e.g. test vs prod).
SILL_DB_CONTAINER=sill_db
POSTGRES_DB=sill
POSTGRES_USER=sill
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=sill_password
# Bump if 5432 collides with another Postgres on this host.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# --- Embeddings ---
# LOCKED at install. Changing the model later requires a schema rebuild
# because vector column types are wired to EMBEDDING_DIMENSION via
# the app.embedding_dimension Postgres GUC.
# First boot downloads ~1.2 GB of float32 weights (the "300m" is parameters).
EMBEDDING_MODEL_ID=unsloth/embeddinggemma-300m
EMBEDDING_DIMENSION=768
# SILL_EMBEDDINGS_CONTAINER=sill_embeddings

# Serving image and its architecture. The defaults are the amd64 release
# line, which is what the versioned `cpu-N.N` tags are published for.
#
# On an arm64 host (Apple Silicon) that image runs emulated, and first boot
# is the slowest thing in the install — 903 s from container start to first
# healthy probe was observed on an M-series Mac, past install.sh's default
# wait. Upstream also publishes a native arm64 line, which removes the
# emulation:
#
#   EMBEDDING_IMAGE=ghcr.io/huggingface/text-embeddings-inference:cpu-arm64-latest
#   EMBEDDING_PLATFORM=linux/arm64
#
# The tradeoff is version pinning, and it is a real one. There is no
# `cpu-arm64-1.8` — the arm64 line is built from main, not from the tagged
# releases, so `cpu-arm64-latest` moves under you. Pin an immutable
# `cpu-arm64-sha-<sha>` tag instead if reproducibility matters more to you
# than staying current.
#
# What this does NOT buy you is the GPU: containers on macOS get no Metal
# access, so both images are CPU-only there and the win is purely the
# emulation tax. For Metal you have to run the embedding server on the host
# (`brew install text-embeddings-inference`) and repoint
# `embedding_config.service_url` in the database at it — same /embed API,
# no schema change. Note that verify.sh check 1 asserts every Compose
# service is healthy, so it will go red if you stop the container.
#
# Switching between these two images is safe for an existing store: same
# model, same float32 weights, same vector space, no re-embedding. Switching
# to a *different* model or quantization is not — see docs/concepts.md.
# EMBEDDING_IMAGE=ghcr.io/huggingface/text-embeddings-inference:cpu-1.8
# EMBEDDING_PLATFORM=linux/amd64

# --- RabbitMQ ---
RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_USER=sill
RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_PASS=sill_password
RABBITMQ_PORT=5672
# Host ports must be <= 65535.
RABBITMQ_MANAGEMENT_PORT=15672
# SILL_RABBITMQ_CONTAINER=sill_rabbitmq

# --- Maintenance worker ---
# SILL_MAINTENANCE_WORKER_CONTAINER=sill_maintenance_worker

# --- Hook environment (read by plugin/hooks/*) ---
# Path to an installed episodic-memory plugin. Unset = degrade gracefully.
# SILL_EPISODIC_MEMORY_PATH=
